# Safety invariants (apply to any project)

The project defaults ("least code," "avoid premature abstraction," "flat
structure") are correct almost everywhere. This file defines the exceptions:
places where minimalism must yield to explicitness, and a default behavior for
working without a test suite or a second developer to catch regressions.

## 1. Trust boundaries and money logic are never simplified away

Some code exists specifically to prevent a costly failure, not because of
over-engineering. Do not remove, inline, or "simplify" a step in:

- Any calculation that determines a price, cost, discount, tax, payout, or
  balance shown to a user or used for a transaction.
- Any input validation at a boundary where the caller is external, untrusted,
  or a different service/process (API request bodies, webhook payloads, form
  submissions, file uploads, query params).
- Any authentication or authorization check.
- Any step that prevents duplicate writes (idempotency keys, unique
  constraints, dedupe checks) on mutations that create records or move money/state.

If a task seems to require touching one of these, treat "fewer lines" as a
non-goal for that specific change. Prefer one extra explicit line over a
clever one-liner that silently drops a check. When in doubt, ask before
removing a check rather than assuming it was dead code.

## 2. One canonical implementation per business rule

If the same calculation, formula, or business rule could plausibly be
duplicated across files, layers, or repos (for resilience, caching, or
offline fallback), identify which copy is canonical and which is a fallback,
and say so explicitly in the change. Never let a "fallback" or "cached" copy
silently drift from the canonical one — if you change the canon, call out
that the fallback needs the same change as a separate, visible step, even
if updating it is out of scope for the current task.

## 3. Layered auth/gating must be checked for ordering conflicts

When a codebase has more than one way to authenticate a request (e.g. session
cookie for browser users, API key/bearer token for server-to-server calls,
webhook signing secret for third-party callbacks), a shared middleware or
guard can accidentally gate all of them with the wrong check first — silently
breaking one auth path while looking correct for the others.

Whenever adding, editing, or reordering middleware/guards that sit in front of
multiple routes:
- List which routes use which auth mechanism before changing shared logic.
- Confirm machine-to-machine and webhook routes are not blocked by a check
  meant only for interactive/browser sessions, and vice versa.
- If unsure which routes are affected, ask rather than assuming the existing
  matcher/config is exhaustive.

## 4. Infrastructure swaps near auth/session/payment code require an explicit warning, not a silent change

Changes framed as "performance" or "best practice" — swapping a DB driver,
connection pooling strategy, ORM adapter, session store, or serialization
format — can break authentication or data integrity in ways that only show up
in production, not locally. Before making such a change near auth, session, or
payment-adjacent code:
- State plainly what could break and why (e.g. "this changes how sessions are
  read; recommend testing login before merging to main").
- Prefer proposing the change as a separate, isolated step the user can test
  and revert independently, rather than bundling it with an unrelated fix.

## 5. Cross-service and cross-repo contracts are called out, never assumed synced

If a change touches a request/response shape, environment variable name,
shared secret, or webhook payload that crosses a boundary into another
service, repo, or deployment (even one not currently open in the editor):
- Name explicitly which other codebase(s) also need a matching update.
- Do not assume another repo will "just pick up" the change — treat every
  other service as being on its own release cycle unless told otherwise.
- Do not rename or restructure a shared field "for clarity" without flagging
  that every consumer of that field needs the same update.

## 6. Default behavior when there is no test suite and no second reviewer

Many of these projects are maintained solo, often by someone without a deep
engineering background, with no automated tests and no code review. Compensate
for that directly:

- Before editing any file that touches auth, payments/pricing, database
  migrations, middleware, or a cross-service contract, state in plain language
  what could break and for whom (which user, which workflow) — even if not
  asked. The explanation is the safety net that a test suite or reviewer would
  normally provide.
- Prefer changes that are easy to verify manually in under a minute (a specific
  input to try, a specific screen to check) over changes whose correctness can
  only be confirmed by reading the code.
- When fixing a narrow bug, do not opportunistically refactor, rename, or
  reformat surrounding code in the same change — a wider diff is harder for a
  non-specialist to sanity-check.
- If the project has a running log of past incidents or "do not do X, it broke
  Y before" notes (in any file, e.g. `OPS.md`, `KNOWN-ISSUES.md`, README, or
  project-specific rules), treat it as higher priority than general best
  practice and check it before touching related code.
